 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** ([Decision doc](decisions/change-delivery-approach.md).) Committed revisions reach browsers over WebSocket connections the project runs itself. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it accepts seconds of lag and repeated reads for a store that would need cursor queries; a hosted realtime service was rejected because it accepts recurring fees and provider coupling for connection capacity the project can own. Self-hosted sockets deliver fast updates without a realtime vendor, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect operations.
